-- title:
--   Regular local rings are regular rings: every localization at a prime is regular local
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a regular local ring, that is, a Noetherian local ring whose maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ can be generated by $\dim R$ elements (Mathlib's `IsRegularLocalRing`, which includes `IsLocalRing` and `IsNoetherianRing`). Then $R$ is a regular ring: for every prime ideal $\mathfrak p \subset R$ the localization $R_{\mathfrak p}$ is again a regular local ring,
--
--   $$\mu\big(\mathfrak p R_{\mathfrak p}\big) = \dim R_{\mathfrak p} \quad\text{for every prime } \mathfrak p \subset R,$$
--
--   where $\mu$ denotes the minimal number of generators.
--
--   Role: this is Serre's theorem that regularity localizes (Matsumura, Theorem 19.3), a consequence of his homological characterization of regular local rings. It shows that regularity of a Noetherian ring can be tested at its maximal ideals, so that a locally Noetherian scheme is regular as soon as its closed points are. Mathlib's `RegularLocalRing/Defs.lean` lists it as a TODO.
--
--   **Formalization Note**: `IsRegularRing R` is Mathlib's class: `R` is Noetherian and `Localization.AtPrime p` is `IsRegularLocalRing` for every prime ideal `p` of `R`, so the statement is equivalent to `∀ (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime], IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p)`.
-- source:
--   H. Matsumura, Commutative Ring Theory, Cambridge Stud. Adv. Math. 8, Cambridge University Press 1986, doi:10.1017/CBO9781139171762, Theorem 19.3; J.-P. Serre, Sur la dimension homologique des anneaux et des modules noethériens, Proc. Int. Symp. Algebraic Number Theory, Tokyo-Nikko 1955, Sci. Council Japan (1956), 175-189

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

theorem IsRegularLocalRing.isRegularRing (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R] :
    IsRegularRing R := by sorry
